Mycelium qa5 third-party notice review
====================================

Executable source: 10a3c18ebea8b9f3f7be981657957ac10a5a2f16
Executable SHA256: 60664012ba7b7b3a7c3ece05975de582caa9b6cc207a4f1b3ce535f17d3c2fa8
Cargo.lock SHA256: 1577e65d674b4ddfd132efec0bf11df0bc87ed1ec30c74bf843b01b342a42544
Notice packager source: f0980fba00412ed0772effc9a80d2d2446f3108b

Scope and selection
-------------------
These notices are a deliberately conservative source-closure superset, not a
claim that all listed components are linked into this executable. No exclusion
is made solely because a component is target-specific, build-only, a procedural
macro, or inactive. The package's MIT OR Apache-2.0 license applies to first-party
Mycelium code, not to the independently licensed code named in these notices.
Additional notices do not change the executable's license or imply that compiler
licenses apply to the application.

The retained inventory covers all 500 exact registry source versions and their
available filename-selected and file-local attribution evidence. Missing archive
notices were supplemented from the exact published source revisions. Complete
original sources remain part of the corresponding-source inputs; compacted
notices do not replace them. Ambiguous comment boundaries and files with later
attribution markers retain their complete original source file.

The actual build used Rust 1.98.1 and cargo-zigbuild 0.23.4/Zig 0.14.1, targeting
x86_64-unknown-linux-musl with mycelium-cli's iroh-sync feature. Native code
includes AWS-LC 5.7.0 (aws-lc-sys 0.45.0), ring 0.17.14's BoringSSL code and
Lua 5.4.8 (lua-src 550.0.0). Their bundled notices remain verbatim. LuaJIT and
other source-only Lua versions are not asserted to be selected runtimes.

Rust library COPYRIGHT-library.html and underlying runtime notices are retained.
COPYRIGHT.html covers the whole compiler and is additional source/build evidence,
not a statement that every compiler component is part of the application. Zig's
musl, compiler_rt, libunwind and other materialized library notices are retained
conservatively. Rust's exact LLVM libunwind submodule is retained separately.
Ubuntu Zig and LLVM header source packages have authenticated exact source
closures; headers are not relabelled as Zig-authored code.

Declaration-only upstream exceptions
------------------------------------
enum-assoc 1.4.1, source revision
351b306939b150601451df752a183b077b0de85a, explicitly declares MIT OR Apache-2.0
in its Cargo manifest. It is a procedural macro. Its exact declaration and author
metadata are retained; standard MIT and Apache-2.0 texts are labelled supplements.
No upstream LICENSE file or copyright statement is invented.

pharos 0.5.3, source revision
f5b985a59cfeced5ae66325d477b6c982c9408bf, explicitly declares Unlicense in its
manifest and exact README. The declarations and labelled standard Unlicense text
are retained. No upstream license file is falsely attributed to the author.

For both exceptions, exact revisions and full fetched histories were examined.
Absence of a separate license filename is not substituted for an absent license
declaration: these two have explicit declarations. This review accepts the
documented declaration evidence for this bounded engineering coverage inventory;
it is not legal certification or a replacement for upstream ownership evidence.

Admission and distribution boundary
----------------------------------
The reviewed input must retain both declarations, their clearly labelled texts,
all collected nonempty notices, the dependency license inventory and this review.
The packager verifies the binary, lock, inventory and every retained notice hash
before producing output, then verifies its staged copies. Reviewed-complete
means this explicit conservative notice scope has been reviewed; it does not
mean legal clearance is asserted or that textual scanning proves all obligations.

Notice-only repackaging must leave the qualified executable bytes unchanged and
record its source separately from the newer packager's source. A new package and
ISO require their own hashes and artifact checks. The Debian/media source section
is independently documented in MEDIA-LICENSE-COVERAGE.md. Release signatures,
public source availability and publication are separate gates.
